Stop nesting Tcl_Eval before the stack runs out (docs/M1-REPORT.md,
section 7.3).

Tiny Tcl stops at MAX_NESTING_DEPTH (100) nested Tcl_Eval calls, but on an
8086 a level costs one to two kilobytes of stack, so a 32 KB stack is gone
after about 15 levels of a recursive proc. Open Watcom's stack check, at
function entry and with a small margin, does not cover the C library,
which is compiled without it: sprintf and its kind then run below the
stack and overwrite DGROUP (seen as a corrupt MCB chain and writes into
the ROM BIOS under DOSBox-X). Tcl_Eval now also fails, with a Tcl error,
when TclStackExhausted() says that less than a safe margin is left; tcl16
supplies it (src/stack.c).

diff -ru a/tclBasic.c b/tclBasic.c
--- a/tclBasic.c
+++ b/tclBasic.c
@@ -506,6 +506,19 @@
     }
 
     /*
+     * tcl16: and stop before the stack runs out. Each level costs one to
+     * two kilobytes of an 8086's stack, so MAX_NESTING_DEPTH alone does
+     * not keep it within its segment, and the C library's own functions
+     * do no stack checking (docs/M1-REPORT.md, section 7.3).
+     */
+
+    if (TclStackExhausted()) {
+	iPtr->numLevels--;
+	iPtr->result =  "too many nested calls to Tcl_Eval (out of stack)";
+	return TCL_ERROR;
+    }
+
+    /*
      * Initialize the area in which command copies will be assembled.
      */
 
diff -ru a/tclInt.h b/tclInt.h
--- a/tclInt.h
+++ b/tclInt.h
@@ -656,6 +656,7 @@
 			    char *procName));
 extern int		TclGetFrame _ANSI_ARGS_((Tcl_Interp *interp,
 			    char *string, CallFrame **framePtrPtr));
+extern int		TclStackExhausted _ANSI_ARGS_((void));
 extern int		TclGetLong _ANSI_ARGS_((Tcl_Interp *interp,
 			    char *string, long *longPtr));
